   "text": "I read the drawing as a linear peptide with one disulfide bridge, written N-terminus to C-terminus.\n\n**Sequence as drawn:** Ac-Ile-Cys-Val-Trp(1-Me)-Gln-Asp-Trp-Gly-Ala-His-Arg-Cys-Thr, followed by a linker and a lysine amide.\n\n- **N-terminus:** acetylated isoleucine.\n- **Disulfide:** the two Cys side chains are joined S\u2013S. In the SMILES this is a ring closure between the two sulfurs.\n- **Methylated Trp:** the first Trp has a methyl on the indole nitrogen (N1). The second Trp has a free indole N\u2013H.\n- **Other side chains:** Gln (amide side chain), Asp (free carboxylic acid), His (imidazole), and Arg (guanidine).\n- **C-terminal side:** the Thr carbonyl is joined to \u2013NH\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)\u2013 (a short ether-chain linker), which is joined to the \u03b1-amine of a lysine. The lysine ends as a primary amide, \u2013C(=O)NH2.\n- **Lysine side chain:** the \u03b5-nitrogen carries a wavy bond to a carbon with two short stubs. I read this as an isopropyl-like group, \u2013NH\u2013CH(CH3)2. It may really be an attachment point to something not shown, so this part is uncertain.\n- **Stereochemistry:** I assumed all residues are the natural L-amino acids (L-Ile, L-Thr and so on) and wrote the stereocentres to match, without checking each wedge or hash in the drawing against that assumption.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)CC)C(=O)N[C@@H](CS1)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2cn(C)c3ccccc23)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCC(N)=O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2c[nH]c3ccccc23)C(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@@H](C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2c[nH]cn2)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCNC(=N)N)C(=O)N[C@@H](CS1)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)C(=O)NCCOCCOCC(=O)N[C@@H](CCCCNC(C)C)C(=O)N"
